# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Crime production resource groups have a `CanNotDelete` lock called `crime-auto-lock`. It stops resources in the group being deleted by mistake or by automation. Updates still work as normal.

The lock doesn't protect data inside a resource. Soft delete and immutable backups cover that.

Code and selection rules: [hmcts/cpp-azure-resource-locks](https://github.com/hmcts/cpp-azure-resource-locks).

## What's locked

Every 3 hours the [enable pipeline](https://dev.azure.com/hmcts-cpp/cpp-apps/_build?definitionId=400) locks production resource groups in these subscriptions:

- Strategic Platform - live
- MoJ-HMCTS-Crime-IdAM-Live

These groups are never locked:

- PRX and PRP groups
- Azure-managed groups, such as AKS `MC_*` groups
- Groups holding an AKS cluster, a VM scale set or VM restore points
- Groups with nothing stateful in them
- Groups tagged `exemptFromAutoLock=true` or listed in the repo's exclusions

The [README](https://github.com/hmcts/cpp-azure-resource-locks#what-gets-locked) has the full rules.

To check whether a group is locked:

```shell
az lock list -g <resource group> --subscription "<subscription>" -o table
```

## What a lock blocks

Any delete inside a locked group fails with `ScopeLocked`:

```text
The scope '...' cannot perform delete operation because following scope(s) are locked: '/subscriptions/.../resourceGroups/<group>'. Please remove the lock and try again.
```

This includes Terraform removing or replacing a child resource, not only deleting a whole resource. Common cases:

- a subnet, NSG rule, route or private endpoint
- a NIC, disk or DNS record
- a role assignment

A Terraform plan that shows `destroy` or `-/+ replace` for anything in a locked group will fail at apply.

## Unlock a group

Use this when a change needs to delete something in a locked group.

1. Run the [unlock pipeline](https://dev.azure.com/hmcts-cpp/cpp-apps/_build?definitionId=822) from `main`:
    - **Subscription**: Strategic Platform - live or MoJ-HMCTS-Crime-IdAM-Live
    - **Resource groups**: comma separated, for example `RG-PRD-XXX-01,RG-PRD-YYY-01`
1. Ask someone else in **CPP DevOps SC** to approve it. You can't approve your own run.
1. Once it's finished, apply your change straight away.
1. Do nothing else. The lock comes back on the next scheduled run, within 3 hours.

Unlock just before you apply. The next run can come sooner than you expect and lock the group again.

The unlock pipeline only removes `crime-auto-lock`. It leaves other locks alone, such as Postgres server locks and `AzureBackupLock-DoNotDelete`.

## Stop a group being locked

If a group should never be locked, for example because it's rebuilt or cleaned up regularly:

1. Add the tag `exemptFromAutoLock=true` to the group in the Terraform that manages it. Say why in the PR.
1. If the group is already locked, run the unlock pipeline once. The tag stops new locks but doesn't remove an existing one.

## Lock a group that's skipped

To lock a group the rules skip, raise a PR in [cpp-azure-resource-locks](https://github.com/hmcts/cpp-azure-resource-locks) adding it to `config/included-resource-groups.txt`. PRX and PRP groups can't be locked this way.

## Who can remove a lock

The unlock pipeline is the expected route. Anyone with Owner or User Access Administrator on the subscription can also delete a lock directly, through the portal or CLI. Don't do this: it bypasses the approval, and the next scheduled run puts the lock back anyway.

## Troubleshooting

| Problem | What to do |
|---|---|
| Apply fails with `ScopeLocked` | [Unlock the group](#unlock-a-group), then apply again. |
| Group locked again before you applied | The scheduled run re-locked it. Unlock again and apply straight after approval. |
| Unlock run is waiting | It needs approval from someone else in CPP DevOps SC. |
| Group still locked after an unlock | Check the run's log for the group. Only `crime-auto-lock` is removed, so another lock may still be there: `az lock list -g <group>`. |
| Tagged group still locked | The tag only stops new locks. Run the unlock pipeline once. |
| Enable run failed | Check the run log. It fails rather than guess when a config file is empty or Azure returns nothing. The next run retries. |
